---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Integrating omics measured on different individuals" toc={false}>
<style>
  .hero { padding: 4.5rem 0 1rem; max-width: 46rem; }
  .hero h1 { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .hero .lede { font-size: 1.2rem; }
  .fig { margin: 3rem 0 1rem; }
  .fig svg { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }
  .cap { color: var(--fg-3); font-size: .84rem; max-width: 44rem; margin: .6rem 0 0; }
  .band { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-top: 4rem; padding-top: 3rem; }
  .band > h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .prose { max-width: 44rem; }
  .dot { animation: pulse 4s ease-in-out infinite; }
  @keyframes pulse { 0%,100% { opacity: .55 } 50% { opacity: 1 } }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .dot { animation: none; opacity: .85 } }
  .flow { stroke-dasharray: 4 5; animation: dash 5s linear infinite; stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  @keyframes dash { to { stroke-dashoffset: -45 } }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .flow { animation: none } }
  /* var() does not resolve inside SVG presentation attributes, so fills and
     strokes that follow the theme are set as CSS properties instead. */
  .fig svg text { font-family: var(--sans); }
  .fig .t-strong { fill: var(--fg); font-weight: 600; }
  .fig .t-soft   { fill: var(--fg-2); }
  .fig .t-mute   { fill: var(--fg-3); }
  .fig .t-mono   { fill: var(--fg-3); font-family: var(--mono); }
  .fig .dot      { fill: var(--accent); }
  .fig .panel    { fill: var(--bg-2); stroke: var(--rule); }
  .fig .panel-hi { fill: var(--bg-2); stroke: var(--accent); }
  .fig .stem     { stroke: var(--rule); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .fig .stem-hi  { stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.5; }
</style>

<section class="hero">
  <p class="eyebrow">R package · disjoint-cohort multi-omics</p>
  <h1>Omics measured on different individuals, integrated through named biology.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Measuring several omics layers on the same individuals is rarely possible: the assay consumes
    the sample, each platform needs its own preparation, and the layers of a study usually come
    from different groups at different times. CHORALE scores every modality on one fixed vocabulary
    of named biological concepts, then asks two questions of it. Whether the design of each study
    supports a named concept, and what low-rank state is supported by the collection as a whole.
  </p>
  <div class="btnrow">
    <a class="btn" href={base + '/tutorial'}>Run the tutorial</a>
    <a class="btn ghost" href={base + '/why'}>Why it exists</a>
    <a class="btn ghost" href="https://github.com/lescailab/chorale">Source</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="fig reveal">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 900 372" role="img" aria-label="Three cohorts with no individual in common are each scored on one fixed vocabulary of named concepts. The concept scores then branch: on one side the design of each study gives an adjusted phenotype effect per concept and the effects are combined; on the other every sample is stacked against every concept and the observed entries are fitted with one low-rank model.">
    <defs>
      <linearGradient id="g" x1="0" x2="1">
        <stop offset="0" style="stop-color:var(--accent);stop-opacity:.14"/>
        <stop offset=".5" style="stop-color:var(--accent);stop-opacity:.4"/>
        <stop offset="1" style="stop-color:var(--accent);stop-opacity:.14"/>
      </linearGradient>
    </defs>

    {[0,1,2].map((i) => {
      const x = 90 + i * 310;
      const label = ['transcriptome','proteome','lipidome'][i];
      const n = ['cohort A','cohort B','cohort C'][i];
      return (
        <g>
          <text x={x + 85} y="20" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" class="t-strong">{label}</text>
          <text x={x + 85} y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="t-mono">{n}</text>
          {[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7].map((j) => (
            <circle class="dot" cx={x + 12 + (j % 4) * 48} cy={58 + Math.floor(j / 4) * 18}
                    r="5" style={`animation-delay:${(i * 8 + j) * 0.18}s`} />
          ))}
          <line class="stem" x1={x + 85} y1="86" x2={x + 85} y2="104"/>
        </g>
      );
    })}

    <rect x="60" y="104" width="780" height="44" rx="8" class="panel-hi"/>
    <text x="450" y="124" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" class="t-strong">one vocabulary of named concepts, fixed before the run</text>
    <text x="450" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="t-soft">curated sets of features, the same sets for every modality</text>

    {[0,1,2].map((i) => {
      const x = 90 + i * 310;
      return (
        <g>
          <line class="stem-hi" x1={x + 85} y1="148" x2={x + 85} y2="170"/>
          <rect x={x} y="170" width="170" height="52" rx="8" class="panel"/>
          <text x={x + 85} y="191" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11.5" class="t-soft">concept scores</text>
          <text x={x + 85} y="209" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11.5" class="t-mute">one per concept, per sample</text>
          <line class="stem" x1={x + 85} y1="222" x2={x + 85} y2="252"/>
        </g>
      );
    })}

    <line class="flow" x1="175" y1="250" x2="725" y2="250"/>

    {/* The scores branch here: one side reads each modality and combines the
        answers, the other is estimated from every sample at once. */}
    <line class="stem-hi" x1="220" y1="250" x2="220" y2="264"/>
    <line class="stem-hi" x1="680" y1="250" x2="680" y2="264"/>

    <rect x="40" y="264" width="360" height="46" rx="8" class="panel-hi"/>
    <text x="220" y="284" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" class="t-strong">the design tests each concept, modality by modality</text>
    <text x="220" y="300" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" class="t-soft">one adjusted effect per concept, then combined</text>

    <rect x="500" y="264" width="360" height="46" rx="8" class="panel-hi"/>
    <text x="680" y="284" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" class="t-strong">every sample against every concept, decomposed once</text>
    <text x="680" y="300" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" class="t-soft">loadings common to the collection by construction</text>

    <line class="stem" x1="220" y1="310" x2="220" y2="326"/>
    <line class="stem" x1="680" y1="310" x2="680" y2="326"/>

    <rect x="20" y="326" width="400" height="36" rx="9" fill="url(#g)"/>
    <text x="220" y="349" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5" class="t-strong">concepts the design supports</text>
    <rect x="480" y="326" width="400" height="36" rx="9" fill="url(#g)"/>
    <text x="680" y="349" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5" class="t-strong">a state fitted across the collection</text>
  </svg>
  <p class="cap">
    No individual appears twice, and the cohorts need not be the same size. Two modalities need
    share neither an individual nor a feature to be compared on how far a named piece of biology
    moves with the phenotype in each of them. The concept scores then branch. On the left each
    modality is read separately and the answers combined, so what is estimated is a quantity a
    modality could have estimated alone. On the right every sample is stacked against every concept
    and fitted once, which estimates directions from more samples and broader concept coverage than
    any single modality contributes.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="band prose reveal">
  <h2>What the method gives where the same individuals were never measured twice</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">01</span>
      <h3>A concept is the same concept everywhere</h3>
      <p>Its name and its members are fixed before the data are seen, so the modalities meet in one
         space and no correspondence between them has to be estimated.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">02</span>
      <h3>A few hundred stated hypotheses</h3>
      <p>Each modality is asked whether a named concept changes with the configured phenotype, under
         family-wise control across the whole vocabulary and a false discovery rate estimated over
         the same permutations.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">03</span>
      <h3>Biology outside the vocabulary</h3>
      <p>The concept scores are removed from each assay and what remains is decomposed into free
         dimensions. A free dimension that moves with the phenotype is coordinated variation the
         vocabulary has no name for.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">04</span>
      <h3>A state estimated from the collection</h3>
      <p>The concept scores of every modality share their columns, so they stack into one matrix of
         every sample against every concept and are decomposed once. The direction that comes out
         uses the samples and observed concept coverage of the whole collection. It is a fitted
         low-rank summary, read through its loadings, its stability and its transfer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">05</span>
      <h3>Tested where it was not estimated</h3>
      <p>Each modality is held out, the state is estimated from the rest, and the held-out cohort is
         projected onto loadings carrying none of it: not its samples, batches, assay or
         preparation. This tests portability beyond the assay structure used for fitting.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">06</span>
      <h3>Concepts tested as groups</h3>
      <p>A change spread across a dozen overlapping concepts can leave every one below threshold
         while the group is unambiguous. Families are formed from the vocabulary before anything is
         tested, and scored on the directional sum across their members.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">07</span>
      <h3>Coverage before results</h3>
      <p><code>chorale_concepts()</code> reports how many features each modality has for each
         concept before anything is fitted, so the size of the claim is known in advance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">08</span>
      <h3>A calibration that keeps the design</h3>
      <p>The null holds the design fixed and exchanges the part of a score the covariates do not
         explain, and one permutation serves every concept, so concepts sharing features stay
         dependent in the null as they are in the data.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band prose reveal">
  <h2>What a result looks like</h2>
  <p>
    One row per concept, carrying the modalities that express it, the effect combined across them,
    and the error rates the vocabulary was read at. This is the first table the report writes,
    as <code>concepts.tsv</code>.
  </p>

<div class="tablewrap">

| concept | modalities | joint_z | sign_agreement | p_family | q_value |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| REACTOME_COMPLEMENT_CASCADE | RNA, PROT, METAB | 5.12 | TRUE | 0.004 | 0.01 |
| HALLMARK_OXIDATIVE_PHOSPHORYLATION | RNA, PROT | -4.03 | TRUE | 0.031 | 0.04 |

</div>

  <p>
    <code>p_family</code> is read against the largest statistic anywhere in the vocabulary, so it
    accounts for every concept the run scored rather than the ones it reported.
    <code>sign_agreement</code> says whether the modalities moved the same way: a concept whose
    layers disagree in direction has not been recovered in common, whatever the size of its effects.
  </p>
  <p>
    Alongside it, <code>chorale_concept_specificity()</code> puts each covariate the modalities
    share in the phenotype's place, on the terms that covariate contributes, which separates a
         concept that tracks the phenotype from one tracking something the cohorts differ in.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="band prose reveal">
  <h2>What the collection knows and its members do not</h2>
  <p>
    Combining per-modality effects is a meta-analysis: the modality is the unit of estimation, and
    what is estimated is a number each modality could in principle have estimated alone.
    <code>chorale_joint_state()</code> estimates something else. Every modality is described on the
    same vocabulary, so the score matrices share their columns and stack by row into one matrix of
    every sample against every concept, decomposed once.
  </p>
  <p>
    The result is a fitted direction over the whole vocabulary. Stacking increases the number and
    diversity of observed rows and allows concepts with incomplete coverage to contribute where
    they are measured, which makes a direction over the whole vocabulary estimable. A concept a
    modality cannot express is missing rather than zero: after centring
    within modality, zero would be a claim that the modality measured exactly average, so the entry
    carries no weight instead and the narrowest layer never bounds the vocabulary.
  </p>

<div class="tablewrap">

| held out | trained on | component | loading_agreement | z | p_value |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| RNA | PROT, LIPID, METAB | joint_04 | 0.91 | 5.97 | 0.0002 |
| PROT | RNA, LIPID, METAB | joint_05 | 0.88 | -6.21 | 0.0002 |

</div>

  <p>
    <code>chorale_joint_transfer()</code> holds each modality out, estimates the state from the
    rest, and projects the held-out cohort onto loadings containing none of it. A permutation asks
    whether structure could arise by chance under this design; transfer asks whether it survives in
    a cohort sharing no individual, batch, assay or preparation with the one that produced it. What
    survives that is portability beyond modality-specific structure, measured as an association
    across cohorts.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="band prose reveal">
  <h2>Built for the data that exists</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Deposited cohorts</h3>
      <p>Public transcriptomes, proteomes and lipidomes for one condition, generated by
         different groups on different individuals.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Two columns required</h3>
      <p>A sample identifier and a phenotype. Every other column is a covariate, used where the
         modalities share it and dropped where they do not.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Tens to hundreds of samples</h3>
      <p>Statistics estimable at the cohort sizes bulk multi-omics resources actually reach, with
         precision that grows with samples rather than with design strata.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band prose reveal">
  <h2>The whole workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Three matrices, three sample tables, one call. <code>chorale_load()</code> returns a
    <code>SummarizedExperiment</code> per modality, and everything after the fit reads results
    rather than configuring the method.
  </p>
<pre><code>containers &lt;- Map(chorale_load, matrices, designs)

concepts &lt;- chorale_concepts(containers,
                            sets          = chorale_genesets(species = "Mus musculus"),
                            feature_space = c(METAB = "lipid"))

fit &lt;- chorale_concept_fit(containers, concepts, n_free = "auto")

fit                                   # the concepts the design supports
chorale_free_dimensions(fit)          # what moved with the phenotype unnamed
chorale_concept_specificity(fit)      # whether another covariate does the same

families &lt;- chorale_concept_families(concepts)
family_result &lt;- chorale_family_evidence(fit$evidence, families)

state &lt;- chorale_joint_state(fit$encoding, nuisance = list(RNA = "study"))
joint_result &lt;- chorale_joint_evidence(state)
transfer &lt;- chorale_joint_transfer(fit$encoding)

chorale_report(
  fit, chorale_null(fit, containers), "results/",
  family_evidence = family_result,
  joint_state = state,
  joint_evidence = joint_result,
  joint_transfer = transfer
)</code></pre>
  <p>
    The <a href={base + '/tutorial'}>tutorial</a> runs this end to end on simulated data and explains
    every number it prints.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="band prose reveal">
  <h2>Install</h2>
<pre><code># install.packages("devtools")
devtools::install_github("lescailab/chorale")</code></pre>
  <p>
    R 4.5 or later. Bioconductor dependencies resolve through <code>BiocManager</code>.
    The <a href={base + '/tutorial'}>tutorial</a> runs end to end on simulated data, so nothing
    needs downloading to see the whole workflow.
  </p>
</section>
</Base>
